writing:{
 title:'Task 2 – Discussion (band 7, danh từ hóa)',
 prompt:'Some people believe that the widespread use of navigation apps is making people less able to find their way. Others think that these apps simply allow people to use their time and skills more effectively. <b>Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</b> Write at least 250 words.',
 min:250,max:330,
 wex:[
  ['Câu danh từ hóa đúng:',['The availability of navigation apps has led to a decline in the use of paper maps.','The available of apps has led to decline paper maps.','Apps are available so paper maps decline.'],0,'The availability of + a decline in.'],
  ['Giới từ đúng: “our increasing reliance ______ technology”',['on','in','of'],0,'reliance on.'],
  ['Câu thể hiện ý kiến cân bằng band 7:',['Apps are bad.','While navigation apps may weaken certain skills, I believe their benefits are greater, provided that people use them selectively.','I agree with both.'],1,'Nêu ý kiến có điều kiện.'],
  ['Câu dẫn nguồn nghiên cứu tự nhiên:',['Research suggests that regular GPS users may have weaker spatial memory.','Research proves 100% that GPS destroys memory.','I read that GPS is bad.'],0,'Hedging: suggests, may.'],
  ['Câu ví dụ cụ thể nhất:',['For example, some people get lost.','For instance, many delivery drivers in Ho Chi Minh City can now work in unfamiliar districts on their first day because of map apps.','For example, apps are useful.'],1,'Chủ thể + nơi chốn + hệ quả.'],
  ['Từ nối mở thân bài 2 band 7:',['On the other hand,','Conversely, proponents of these apps argue that…','Second,'],1,'Đa dạng + giới thiệu quan điểm.']],
 outline:['Mở bài: 2 quan điểm + ý kiến: lợi ích lớn hơn nếu dùng có chọn lọc.','Thân bài 1: quan điểm “làm yếu kỹ năng” — chú ý giảm, nghiên cứu trí nhớ không gian (hedging: suggests, may).','Thân bài 2: quan điểm “dùng thời gian hiệu quả” — tài xế giao hàng, giảm căng thẳng; kỹ năng thay đổi không mất đi. Ý kiến: tắt app trên đường quen.','Kết bài: nhắc lại ý kiến.'],
 model:'With the rapid spread of smartphones, navigation apps have become an everyday tool for millions of people. While some argue that this <b>reliance on</b> technology is eroding our ability to find our way, others believe that it frees us to use our time and skills more productively. In my view, the benefits of these apps outweigh the drawbacks, provided that they are used selectively.<br><br>Those who are concerned about navigation apps point to <b>a decline in</b> attention. When a device tells us exactly where to turn, we have little reason to notice landmarks or to form a mental map of our surroundings. Research suggests that regular GPS users may have weaker spatial memory than people who navigate independently, and some educators worry that children who always follow a screen may never develop these skills at all. From this perspective, convenience comes at a significant cognitive cost.<br><br>Conversely, proponents of these apps argue that they allow people to focus on more valuable tasks. <i>For instance</i>, many delivery drivers in Ho Chi Minh City can now work efficiently in unfamiliar districts from their first day, which would have been almost impossible a decade ago. <b>The availability of</b> real-time traffic information also reduces stress and saves time. Moreover, skills tend to change rather than disappear; people who use digital maps are often adept at planning complex journeys involving several forms of transport.<br><br>On balance, I believe that navigation apps are a valuable tool rather than a threat. However, since there is evidence that the brain benefits from being challenged, it would be sensible for people to switch them off on familiar routes and to rely on their own sense of direction from time to time.',
 note:'Khoảng 310 từ, band 7.0. Danh từ hóa (reliance on, a decline in, the availability of), hedging khi dẫn nghiên cứu (suggests, may), ví dụ cụ thể (tài xế giao hàng TP.HCM), ý kiến có điều kiện nhất quán.',
 checklist:['Mở bài: 2 quan điểm + ý kiến rõ','Ít nhất 3 cụm danh từ hóa đúng giới từ','Dẫn nghiên cứu có hedging (suggests / may)','Mỗi thân bài có ví dụ cụ thể','Ý kiến riêng nhất quán đến kết bài','≥ 250 từ trong 40 phút']
},
speaking:{
 task:'Ghi âm Part 2 (2 phút) và cả 3 câu Part 3, gửi cô.',
 items:[
 ['Are you good at finding your way?',"Not particularly, to be honest. In my own district I'm fine, but in a new place I rely on my phone almost immediately. My brother, on the other hand, can find his way anywhere."],
 ['Do you use maps on your phone?',"All the time, mainly to check how long a journey will take or to find a café. I almost never use a paper map, although we did learn to read them in geography lessons."],
 ['Have you ever got lost?',"Yes, once in Đà Lạt. My phone battery died, and I spent about an hour walking around in circles before a kind woman selling strawberries pointed me in the right direction."],
 ['Do you think it is important to learn how to read a map?',"I think so, because technology can fail. Also, reading a map gives you a much better sense of where places are in relation to each other."]],
 part2:{cue:'Describe a place you visited where you got lost.',bullets:['where it was','why you went there','how you got lost','and explain how you felt about the experience.'],
  notes:'Đà Lạt, grade 11 trip with friends · phone battery died, foggy, all streets on hills look the same · walked 1 hour, strawberry seller helped · panicked, then laughed; learned to carry power bank + notice landmarks',
  model:"I'm going to talk about the time I got lost in Đà Lạt, a city in the Central Highlands that's famous for its cool weather and flower gardens. I went there with three friends during the summer holiday after grade eleven, and on the second afternoon I decided to walk back to our homestay alone, while my friends stayed at a café. The problem was that I had been relying completely on my phone, and halfway back, the battery died. To make matters worse, it had become very foggy, and because Đà Lạt is built on hills, many of the streets look almost identical, with winding roads and similar pine trees. I walked around for about an hour, and I'm fairly sure I passed the same flower shop three times. Eventually, I stopped at a small stall where a woman was selling strawberries. I explained where I was staying, and she not only gave me directions but also drew a little map on a paper bag. I found the homestay ten minutes later. At the time I felt quite panicked, but looking back, it's one of the funniest memories of the trip. It also taught me a useful lesson: since then, I always carry a power bank, and I try to notice landmarks rather than just staring at my screen."},
 part3:[
 ['How has technology changed the way people travel?',"Enormously. Compared with my parents' generation, we can now book transport, find accommodation and navigate unfamiliar cities entirely through our phones. The availability of real-time information has made travel far more efficient. Arguably, though, it has also made it less spontaneous, because people rarely just wander and explore."],
 ['Do you think children today have less freedom to explore than in the past?',"In many cities, yes. My parents often talk about cycling around their neighbourhood all afternoon without telling anyone where they were. Today, because of heavy traffic and safety concerns, most children are driven everywhere. What concerns me is that they miss out on learning independence."],
 ['Will people still need geography lessons in the future?',"I'd say they'll be more important than ever, but the focus will probably shift. Rather than memorising capital cities, students will need to understand issues such as climate change, migration and urbanisation. Those are things no app can teach you to think critically about."]]
},
