writing:{
 title:'Task 1 – Line graph nhiều đường (band 7: chọn đặc điểm chính)',
 prompt:'The graph below shows water consumption in a Southeast Asian country by sector between 1990 and 2030 (projected). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.',
 chart:{type:'line',title:'Water consumption by sector (billion m³)',unit:'billion m³',max:60,step:10,series:['Agriculture','Industry','Domestic'],cats:['1990','2000','2010','2020','2030*'],data:[[40,46,50,48,45],[5,9,16,24,30],[3,5,8,11,13]]},
 min:150,max:200,
 wex:[
  ['Overview band 7 (2 đặc điểm chính, không số):',['Overall, agriculture used 40 billion m³ in 1990.','Overall, agriculture remained by far the largest consumer of water throughout the period, while industrial use grew most rapidly and is expected to account for a much larger share by 2030.','Overall, water is important.'],1,'Đặc điểm 1: lớn nhất; đặc điểm 2: tăng nhanh nhất.'],
  ['Câu chứng minh đặc điểm 2 tốt nhất:',['Industry was 5, 9, 16, 24 and 30.','Industrial consumption rose sixfold, from 5 billion m³ in 1990 to a projected 30 billion m³ in 2030.','Industry increased.'],1,'Chọn số đầu – cuối + tính bội số.'],
  ['Cách xử lý dữ liệu dự báo (2030*):',['bỏ qua','dùng is expected to / is projected to','viết như đã xảy ra'],1,'Thì tương lai dự báo.'],
  ['Câu mô tả agriculture chính xác:',['Agriculture increased steadily over the whole period.','Agricultural use peaked at 50 billion m³ in 2010 and is projected to fall slightly to 45 billion m³ by 2030.','Agriculture went up and down a lot.'],1,'Đỉnh 2010 rồi giảm nhẹ.'],
  ['Nhóm thân bài hợp lý:',['Thân 1: agriculture; Thân 2: industry + domestic (cùng tăng)','Mỗi năm một đoạn','Thân 1: 1990–2000; Thân 2: 2010–2030'],0,'Nhóm theo xu hướng.'],
  ['Câu so sánh band 7:',['Domestic use was the smallest category throughout, rising from 3 to a projected 13 billion m³, less than half the figure for industry.','Domestic was small.','Domestic use was 13.'],0,'Có so sánh với đường khác.']],
 outline:['Mở bài: The line graph illustrates how much water was consumed by agriculture, industry and households in a Southeast Asian country from 1990 to 2020, with projections to 2030.','Overview: agriculture lớn nhất suốt giai đoạn; industry tăng nhanh nhất (×6), khoảng cách thu hẹp.','Thân bài 1: agriculture 40 → đỉnh 50 (2010) → dự báo 45 (2030).','Thân bài 2: industry 5 → 30 (sixfold); domestic 3 → 13, luôn thấp nhất.'],
 model:'The line graph illustrates the amount of water consumed by agriculture, industry and households in a Southeast Asian country between 1990 and 2020, together with projections for 2030.<br><br><i>Overall</i>, agriculture was by far the largest consumer of water throughout the period, although its use is expected to decline slightly after 2010. <b>The most striking feature</b>, however, is the rapid growth in industrial consumption, which is projected to narrow the gap with agriculture considerably by 2030.<br><br>In 1990, agriculture used 40 billion m³ of water, eight times as much as industry. This figure rose steadily to <b>peak at</b> 50 billion m³ in 2010, before falling slightly to 48 billion m³ in 2020. It <b>is projected to</b> continue this gradual decline, reaching 45 billion m³ by 2030.<br><br><i>By contrast</i>, industrial demand grew at a much faster rate. Starting at just 5 billion m³ in 1990, it more than tripled to 16 billion m³ in 2010 and is expected to reach 30 billion m³ by 2030, <b>a sixfold increase</b> over the period as a whole. Domestic consumption also rose, from 3 to 11 billion m³ between 1990 and 2020, but it remained the smallest category and is forecast to account for only 13 billion m³ in 2030, <b>less than half</b> the figure for industry.',
 note:'Khoảng 210 từ, band 7.0. Overview nêu 2 đặc điểm chính (agriculture lớn nhất; industry tăng nhanh nhất). Chỉ khoảng 12 số liệu, mỗi số đều chứng minh đặc điểm chính. Dự báo dùng is projected to / is expected to / is forecast to.',
 checklist:['Overview 2 đặc điểm chính, không số liệu','Thân bài nhóm theo xu hướng','Mỗi số liệu chứng minh một đặc điểm','Dữ liệu dự báo dùng thì tương lai','Có ngôn ngữ bội số (tripled, sixfold, less than half)','≥ 150 từ trong 20 phút']
},
speaking:{
 task:'Ghi âm Part 2 (2 phút) và cả 3 câu Part 3, gửi cô.',
 items:[
 ['How much water do you drink every day?',"Probably about two litres, mostly plain water. I carry a bottle to school, and I refill it at the water dispenser in the canteen."],
 ['Do you think people waste water in your city?',"Quite a lot, unfortunately. You often see people washing motorbikes or the pavement with a hose for a long time, and leaking pipes are common too."],
 ['Do you prefer swimming in the sea or in a pool?',"The sea, definitely, because there's more space and it feels more natural. But I don't go very often, since the nearest beach is two hours away."],
 ['Has the weather in your area changed in recent years?',"I think so. The rainy season seems more unpredictable, and when it does rain, it's often very heavy, which causes flooding in my district."]],
 part2:{cue:'Describe a time when you had to save water or another resource.',bullets:['what the situation was','when it happened','what you did to save it','and explain how you felt about it.'],
  notes:'drought + saltwater intrusion, visiting grandparents in Bến Tre, dry season 2024 · tap water salty, bought bottled water, rainwater jars · reused washing water for plants, short showers · shocked; realised how lucky we are in the city',
  model:"I'd like to talk about a time when I had to save water while I was staying with my grandparents in Bến Tre, a province in the Mekong Delta. It was during the dry season a couple of years ago, when the region was hit by a severe drought and saltwater intrusion. Because the rivers were so low, seawater had pushed far inland, and the tap water at my grandparents' house became so salty that it was impossible to drink or even use for cooking. Like many families, they had to buy bottled water for drinking, and they relied on large clay jars of rainwater that they had collected months earlier. So we all had to be extremely careful. We took very short showers using a bucket instead of the shower, and the water we used to wash vegetables was saved and poured onto the plants. My grandmother even refused to let anyone wash the motorbike for a month. At first, I found it quite difficult, because in the city I had never thought about how much water I used. But it was a real eye-opener. What struck me most was how calmly my grandparents dealt with it, as if it were a normal part of life. Since then, I've been much more conscious of not wasting water at home."},
 part3:[
 ['Why do some countries have water shortages?',"There are several reasons. Some regions simply have very little rainfall, while in others, the problem is that water is poorly managed, for example through leaking pipes or inefficient irrigation. Climate change is making things worse, because rainfall is becoming less predictable."],
 ['Should water be more expensive to encourage people to save it?',"To some extent, yes. If water is extremely cheap, people have little incentive to fix leaks or take shorter showers. However, water is a basic need, so prices should be tiered: a low price for the essential amount, and higher prices for heavy users."],
 ['Will technology solve the problem of water scarcity?',"It can certainly help. Desalination and better irrigation systems are already making a difference. But I'm not convinced that technology alone is the answer. Unless people change their habits and governments manage resources more carefully, new technology will only delay the problem."]]
},
